<[len] NMORGENSTERN> C. Set your first name by typing
   /nam at the beginning of  a line
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Carl, I was interested in your progress.
  Jax is very expert
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> mostly in real-time programming and 68000 stuff
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I am more into PC DOS stuff.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Any questions?
<[HumanTorch] FIGCHAPTERS> <<< Thought Len was becoming
  our resident MACHII expert!
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I have played with the Mac Forths only a little, Jax
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I have engaged in an ambitious project:
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I am studying Sedgwicks Algorithms.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Like so many textbooks, the examples are in Pascal,
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> so I have to translate them to Forth.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> There are some dilemmas:
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> For example, arrays.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> In Pascal an array is entered via its
  index & you can access
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> the corresponding
  element. In Forth...
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> you put the index on the stack and
  get the address of the element.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> This gives you a certain power. For example,
<[HumanTorch] FIGCHAPTERS> How is this a dilemma
<[HumanTorch] FIGCHAPTERS> ..?
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> to move items in the array up, you can use CMOVE
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> which is a fast primitive, rather than something
   like a[i] = a[i+1] etc.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> The dilemma is, that for my own purposes,
   I have no hesitation
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> in converting in any manner that suits my convenience.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> If, I am writing for experienced Forth
   programmers, I would do the same.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> But if I am trying to show Pascal users the power of Forth
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I would have to take the conversion
   step by step, or I would
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> confuse them.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> ga
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Another problem:
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Pascal, like BASIC, normally indexes arrays from 1 up.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Forth prefers starting with 0, which
   in my opinion is better. But in transferring a Pascal
   algorithm to Forth, you have to make changes in a lot of places,
   and errors can creep in.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Pascal does allow the option of starting
   with 0, but this does not help if the algorithm is already
   written using arrays that start with 1.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> When you convert an algorithm, there
   is less stress & fewer
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> errors to simply transfer it "as is"
   even though the resulting
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Forth is less than optimal.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> One thing that I thought would give me trouble
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> was the use of variables. But they do not offer any major
   problem. What I do is to
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> create a Forth VARIABLE for each variable in Pascal
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Then I study the thing, and decide
   what I can handle better on
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> the stack and what should remain as variables.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I can usually eliminate many variables
   this way but I see no gain
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> in replacing simple A @ and A! with
   a lot of ROT OVER R> >R etc etc for no other reason than
   to get rid of variables.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Some Forth programmers look down on variables, but I think
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> that convenience is the only criterion.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> For arrays, I have a neat array definer.
<[HumanTorch] FIGCHAPTERS> : array create cells allot does> swap cells + ;
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> I call it -BYTE-ARRAY. The usage is as follows:
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> For 1 dimension arrays you write
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> 10 1-DIMENSION 40 -BYTE-ARRAY FOO
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> This creates an array of 10 40-byte elements.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Now 3 FOO puts you on the 3rd element.
   For 2-dimensional arrays you type 3 10 2-DIMENSION 40 -BYTE-ARRAY
   FOO2. It has no protection against out-of-range indices.

Editor's note: here it is:

  0 \ A VERSATILE ARRAY DEFINER                           NLM870919
  1 : -BYTE-ARRAY  ( r c e -- ) ( r c -- addr; or c -- addr)
  2    CREATE  DUP 2OVER , , , ROT 1 MAX * * ALLOT
  3    DOES>   DUP 2+ @ 0= IF 0 -ROT THEN    ( Fix stack if 1-dim)
  4       >R SWAP R@ @ * + R@ 4 + @ * R> 6 + + ;
  5 : 1-DIMENSION  ( c -- 0 c)   0 SWAP ;
  6 : 2-DIMENSION ;
  
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> You can get the effect of Pascal records
   by defining offsets
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Thus:
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> : OFFSET ( n -- ) CREATE , DOES> @ + ;
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Say we define 0 OFFSET .NAME
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> 20 OFFSET ADDRESS
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Then  3 FOO NAME will put in the name field,
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> and 3 FOO .ADDRESS will put you in the address field.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> This usage resembles Pascal syntax
