<[len] NMORGENSTERN> IP is Forth's instruction pointer.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Could you VIEW those words & tell me
   what file they are in?
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Forth's IP is not to be confused with Intel's IP
<[carl] C.STRUYCKEN1> These were the words I was defining myself
<[carl] C.STRUYCKEN1> I have yet to learn what instruction pointers are
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> ok
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Let's say you have defined a word   : FOO  DO-THIS  
DO-THAT ;
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> Now, when you execute FOO, IP points to the first "token" 
in it
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> which is DO-THIS  The address of the
   next "token" which is DO-THAT
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> is pushed onto the return stack, and
   IP skips down a level to the first
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> token in DO-THIS
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> When it gets to the end of DO-THIS,
   the top of the return stack is popped
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> into the IP and DO-THAT is executed. This kind of action
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> is why Forth is called "threaded"  Its action is a little
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> bit like the yarn in a sweater. It
   goes back and forth, in and out.
<[len] NMORGENSTERN> .
